#{[17592186502110 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502110 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502110 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502110 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of the subset $A=\\{3,5\\}$ of $X=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502110 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "658d443c-02c1-52f6-a2b6-cd97f500f835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502110 #edb/ref 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe indicator function of the set $\\{3,5\\}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed92f46f57aefc4d9c7445f044f1b35adb89950c16f331aff35c02445b607c95.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $3$ and $5$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502127 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502127 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502127 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502127 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{1}_{A}(2)=0,\\,{1}_{B}(2)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=0$\n\nIII. $2\\in A\\cup B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502127 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95e868f8-4928-5f0b-9ec7-afed42df8e19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502127 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(2),{1}_{B}(2)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=0,$ we conclude that $2$ does not belong to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
